class="relative container"><div
class="relative lg:w-22/24 mx-auto"><header
class="page__header"><h1 class="page__title">
Castello di Brescia</h1></header></div></div><div
class="sticky navigation-sticky z-10 top-20 lg:top-0"><div
class="navigation-sticky__body"><div
class="lg:container"><div
class="lg:w-22/24 mx-auto"><div
class="relative py-4"><div
class="lg:flex lg:justify-between lg:items-center"><nav
class="navigation navigation--scrollsnap"><ul
class="navigation__list js-navigation-anchor"></ul></nav></div></div></div></div></div></div><section
class="js-section" data-section-id=" " data-section-title=""><div
class="container my-6 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-7 order-last lg:order-none"><h2 class="text-xl xl:text-3xl text-secondary mb-6 lg:mb-12">Perched at the top of the Cidneo Hill, the Castle is one of the most fascinating fortified complexes in Europe, still bearing the traces of the various dominations of the past.</h2><div
class="prose-ground text-primary mb-6 lg:mb-12"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Mastio, the fortified keep on the top of the hill, with its mighty Torre dei Prigionieri (Tower of the Prisoners), the drawbridge and the Mirabella Tower, the last trace of the church of Santo Stefano in Arce overlooking the town, bear witness to the communal period and the Visconti domination. The imposing bastions, with their monumental gate, are testaments to the power of the Republic of Venice – La Serenissima – that ruled the city for about four centuries.</span></p><p>Within its walls, the Castle also houses two museums: the <a
href="https://www.bresciamusei.com/en/museums-and-venues/luigi-marzoli-arms-and-armor-museum/"><em>Luigi Marzoli</em> Arms and Armor Museum</a> and the <a
href="https://www.bresciamusei.com/en/museums-and-venues/museum-of-the-risorgimento-lioness-of-italy-eng/">Museum of the Risorgimento <em>Lioness of Italy</em></a>.</p></div></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-10 lg:col-span-15">
<img
fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="616" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAABAAAAAJoAQAAAADQEa4jAAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAABjSURBVHja7cEBAQAAAIIg/69uSEABAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAO8GNncAAZg16PYAAAAASUVORK5CYII=" class="w-auto lazyload" alt=""   data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE.jpg 1024w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE-768x462.jpg 768w" data-max-width="1024" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1024" data-eio-rheight="616" /><noscript><img
fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" width="1024" height="616" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE.jpg" class="w-auto" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE.jpg 1024w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Catsello-di-Brescia-WISE-768x462.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" data-eio="l" /></noscript></div></div></div></section><section
class="js-section" data-section-id=" " data-section-title=""><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16 xl:my-28"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-2 lg:col-span-22"><div
class="prose-ground-highlighted text-primary-200"><p>We would like to inform our visitors that, during the Mysteria – Festival of Fantastic Cultures, from November 1 through November 10, the castle’s opening hours will be moved to 7:00 a.m. Additionally, from November 5 through November 9, from 6:00 p.m. until closing, entry to the castle will be permitted only to holders of a Mysteria ticket. Thank you for your understanding.</p></div></div></div></div></section><section
class="js-section" data-section-id="The-Castle" data-section-title="The Castle"><div
class="relative border-t border-gray-100"><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-span-6 xl:col-start-2"><h2 class="text-3xl xl:text-5xl text-primary">The Castle</h2></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 xl:col-span-9 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-start-10 order-last lg:order-none"><div
class="prose-ground text-primary-200"><div
class="wi"><div
id="corpo" class="co"><div
class="cont">
<picture><source   type="image/webp" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/unoscudoperlacultura-1.png.webp"><img
decoding="async" width="154" height="247" class="size-full wp-image-108413 aligncenter lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAAAJoAAAD3AQAAAADN7nA+AAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAAAcSURBVFjD7cGBAAAAAMOg+VOf4AZVAQAAAABcAxRDAAFoJQEXAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC" alt="" data-eio="p" data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/unoscudoperlacultura-1.png" data-eio-rwidth="154" data-eio-rheight="247" /></picture><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="154" height="247" class="size-full wp-image-108413 aligncenter" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/unoscudoperlacultura-1.png" alt="" data-eio="l" /></noscript><hr
/><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Castle is home to charming and little-known passages, atmospheres infused with mystery, affording sweeping views over the city, stretching from the Ronchi slopes and the Brescian valleys to the Apennines and the Alps.</span></p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-start-10 lg:col-span-14"><div
class="prose text-primary"><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Stage to many key events in the history of Brescia, including its famous <strong>Dieci giornate </strong>(Ten days), the Castle is today one of Brescia’s most evocative sites, where several elements coexist: evidence of the Roman era, such as the oil warehouses with their Botticino stone tubs, medieval constructions, and a 1909 locomotive, displayed to the delight of younger visitors inside the “Falcone d’Italia” (the Falcon of Italy, one of the Castle’s names).</span></p></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-14 lg:col-start-10"><div
class="relative"><div
class="accordion accordion-737257 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-737257" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">The Ten days of Brescia</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p>In the most delicate passage of the Risorgimento epic, 1848, the people of Brescia organized an underground committee headed by patriot <strong>Tito Speri</strong> and the curate of Serle Don Pietro Boifava. It was the news of the Austrians&#8217; planned collection of a hefty fine imposed on the citizenry after the formation in 1848 of a Provisional Government that sparked the collective rebellion against the Hapsburg rulers on <strong>March 23, 1849</strong>. The spark was also ignited by the conflicting rumors coming from the front, in the second phase of the <strong>First War of independence</strong> (1848-1849), declared by Charles Albert, King of Sardinia, in an attempt to regain, after the events of 1848 freeing it from the Austrians. In fact, misleading reports of victory for the Savoy troops arrived, mixed with royal dispatches about the Piedmontese defeat at Novara (March 23, 1849), which was followed by the abdication of Charles Albert and the signing of the armistice of Vignale (March 24, 1849) between the new king, Victor Emmanuel II, and General Radetzky.</p><p><strong>Brescia</strong>, which rose up trusting in Piedmontese help, chose not to surrender to the Austrians, engaging in resistance for ten very long days, with the involvement of the people, who fought strenuously house by house and behind barricades set up at key points in the city, while the Austrians, entrenched in <strong>the Castle</strong>, bombarded the urban perimeter. The entire city became a theater of war: the city&#8217;s bell towers and towers were used as lookouts and as a base of operations for sharpshooters. Also targeted by Hapsburg shells were the most emblematic symbols of the municipality, such as <strong>the Loggia</strong>, in which the hole caused by an Austrian shell fired from the Castle still remains, at the base of one of the walls of the Vanvitellian Hall. The insurgents, also led by Tito Speri, faced the Austrians in every corner of the city. Barricades were erected at Porta Torrelunga, Contrada San Barnaba, Contrada Sant&#8217;Urbano and many other places. The Brescians also engaged in violent clashes with the Austrians on the Ronchi and at Sant&#8217;Eufemia della Fonte.</p><p>The surrender of the Brescian people did not occur until April 1, 1849, after <strong>Marshal Haynau</strong> had rushed to support the Austrian garrison barricaded in the Castle. On the night of <strong>March 31</strong>, in fact, taking advantage of the <strong>Strada del Soccorso</strong>, a secret and still existing safe-conduct connecting the top of the Castle to the city, new armed garrisons led by Haynau managed to reach the Cidneo hill. The insurrection was snuffed out in blood, with violent repression especially against civilians, who were bent by shootings that lasted until <strong>August 12</strong>, the date of the amnesty desired by Radetzky. Insurgents taken prisoner were locked up in the Castle or in the prison of St. Urban and many of them were shot. Impatience with the Austrian rulers, however, was not abated, so much so that <strong>Tito Speri</strong> animated a new clandestine insurrectionary committee, <span
style="font-weight: 400;">a decision that would cost him his life. <strong>He was hanged in Belfiore</strong>, Mantua, in <strong>1853</strong>.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The lion-like courage with which Brescia fought during the Risorgimento earned the city the title of </span><em><strong>Leonessa d’Italia</strong></em><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, <em>Lioness of Italy</em>, coined by Aleardo Aleardi and taken up by <strong>Giosue Carducci</strong> in his </span><strong><i>Odi Barbare, </i></strong><span
style="font-weight: 400;"><strong>Book V</strong>, a composition from May 1877, closing with these famous verses:</span></p><p
style="text-align: center;"><strong>Rejoicing in the event Brescia received me,<br
/>
Brescia the strong, Brescia the iron-handed,<br
/>
Drenched in the blood of her enemies,<br
/>
Brescia <em>the lioness of Italy</em>.</strong></p></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-737258 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-737258" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline">Historic phases of the Castle of Brescia</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose"><p><strong>&#8211;<b>From geological formation to the first prehistoric settlements</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The summit of the Cidneo Hill is the earliest Brescian settlement. The oldest finds unearthed during excavations date back to the Iron Age.</span><span
style="font-weight: 400;"><br
/>
</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Celtic settlement</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The earliest significant anthropization of the hilltop area dates back to this period, when the Celtic populations, the Ligurians at first and then the Cenomani, used it as a place of worship, perhaps dedicated to the god Bergimus.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Roman period</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">During the Roman period the hill continued to be used as a place of worship. Temples of uncertain dedication were erected whose steps are still visible inside the Weapons Museum</span>.</p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Below the temple are oil warehouses and other containers whose function remains unknown to this day. Other oil warehouses were built underneath the grassy expanse today known as the Mirabella lawn.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Late Antiquity and the </b><b>Longobard</b><b> period</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The basement of the so-called Mirabella Tower probably dates back to the late Imperial Age. An early Christian martyrium has been found, attesting the perpetration of the site’s spiritual vocation even in the Christian period. </span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">After the sackings of the Visigoths, Huns, and Heruli, came a period of Ostrogothic occupation, of which very few documentary and material traces remain but which was very important for the town. Then came the </span><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Longobards</span><span
style="font-weight: 400;">, who for the first time used the hill for defensive purposes, building the first castrum.</span><span
style="font-weight: 400;"><br
/>
</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Late Middle Ages (twelfth–fourteenth century)</b><b><br
/>
</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">In the late Middle Ages, in continuity with the hill’s cultic vocation, a number of churches were built, the most important of which was the church of Santo Stefano in Arce, whose entire lawn-covered base and some elevated structures, including one of the two bell towers (the Mirabella Tower), are still preserved.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Construction of the Mastio also began at this time.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;</strong><b>Visconti and Malatesta periods</b></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">During the rule of the Visconti family the fortification works were rationalised by taking down old buildings and erecting the Mastio above the old Roman temple and oil warehouses. These works were required by Luchino Visconti and his brother Giovanni, who also commissioned the frescoes that are still visible today.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;</strong><b>First Venetian domination</b></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Venetian domination is characterised by important works to modernise the fortified structures, starting from the transformation of an old quadrangular Visconti tower into a more modern round tower, the Torre dei Prigionieri (Tower of the Prisoners). Thanks to the work of Giacomo Coltrino, a new tower known as Torre Coltrina was built to replace an old one that had collapsed.</span><span
style="font-weight: 400;"><br
/>
</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b></b></strong><b>French period</b></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The short-lived French occupation was particularly significant for the Castle of Brescia, since the new rulers’ defensive necessities led them to lay out a state-of-the-art fortification system, specifically called Torre dei Francesi (Tower of the French), below the Mirabella lawn.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Venetian rule and the Napoleonic period</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">It was with the return of the Venetian rule that the Castle underwent its most significant development, with the construction of a new walled enclosure, the widening of the Strada del Soccorso and the construction of the San Pietro, San Marco, San Faustino and Pusterla bastions. The fortress was also equipped with numerous embrasures and gunports, buildings for storing provisions (the Grande Miglio), ovens, barracks, religious buildings, cisterns, artillery stores (the Piccolo Miglio) and armouries.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>Napoleonic and Austrian periods</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">During the Napoleonic period, the Castle, which had been in decline for some time, remained unchanged and was used mainly as a prison.</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">With the transition from French to Austrian occupation, the Castle took on new strategic functions. The Austrian structures concentrated on re-functionalizing the buildings and military barracks, giving the Castle the form of large barracks. The Mastio area, on the other hand, was abandoned.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;</strong><b>Kingdom of Italy</b></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">In 1859 Brescia became part of the new-born Kingdom of Italy. The Castle, taking advantage of the Austrian structures, continued to be used as barracks for the Italian troops. The Mastio was used as a military prison. In the years between the nineteenth and twentieth centuries, thanks to the initiative of Captain Sorellli, restoration work began on the oldest and most precarious buildings, using the inmates of the Mastio as workforce.</span><span
style="font-weight: 400;"><br
/>
</span></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">In the twentieth century, the Castle made its debut as a public park with the 1904 Expo.</span>.</p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">During World War I, some of the Castle’s structures were used for the detention of Austrian prisoners, before going back to being an urban space between the two world wars.</span></p><p><strong>-1943-1945</strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Castle’s function as a city park from the twentieth century to the present day saw a dramatic re-functionalization during the Italian Social Republic, becoming barracks for the Fascist and German militias, while a few buildings were used as political prisons.</span></p><p><strong>&#8211;<b>From the Post-War period to the present day</b></strong></p><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">The Castle has become one of the most loved places by the people of Brescia, who can finally enjoy its interiors. Over the course of the twentieth century there have been numerous redevelopment projects, starting with the arrangement of the zoo in place of the Venetian barracks, which were demolished and removed in the 1980s.</span></p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></section><section
class="js-section" data-section-id="The-Museums-of-the-Castle" data-section-title="The Museums of the Castle"><div
class="relative border-t border-gray-100"><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-span-6 xl:col-start-2"><h2 class="text-3xl xl:text-5xl text-primary">The Museums of the Castle</h2></div><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-15 xl:col-span-9 lg:col-start-2 xl:col-start-10 order-last lg:order-none"></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-9 lg:my-16"><div
class="grid grid-cols-8 lg:grid-cols-24 gap-6"><div
class="col-span-full lg:col-span-14 lg:col-start-10"><div
class="relative"><div
class="accordion accordion-448512 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-448512" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline"> &#8220;Luigi Marzoli&#8221; Arms Museum</p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose">
<img
decoding="async" width="1201" height="807" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-43299 lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAABLEAAAMnAQAAAADpXP0pAAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAACOSURBVHja7cExAQAAAMKg9U9tCy+gAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIC3Ad83AAG2gtehAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC" alt=""   data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi.jpg 1201w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi-768x516.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi-1072x720.jpg 1072w" data-max-width="1201" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1201" data-eio-rheight="807" /><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="1201" height="807" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-43299" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi.jpg" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi.jpg 1201w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi-768x516.jpg 768w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/v-museo-armi-marzoli-7861fotostudio-Rapuzzi-1072x720.jpg 1072w" sizes="(max-width: 1201px) 100vw, 1201px" data-eio="l" /></noscript><p><span
style="font-weight: 400;">Inside the fourteenth-century Mastio Visconteo, the Luigi Marzoli Arms Museum houses one of Europe’s finest collections of antique armour and weapons, displaying Brescia’s time-honoured weaponry tradition, documenting its technological and artistic evolution between the fifteenth and eighteenth centuries. </span></p></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="accordion accordion-448513 border-gray-100 border-t"><span
class="flex items-center cursor-pointer js-toggle" data-toggle-target=".accordion-448513" data-toggle-class-name="is-accordion-open">
<span
class="accordion__icon"></span><p
class="text-lg lg:text-base xl:text-xl text-primary block my-5 hover:underline"> Museum of the Risorgimento <i>Lioness of Italy</i></p>
</span><div
class="accordion__body"><div
class="accordion__content"><div
class="text-primary"><div
class="prose">
<img
decoding="async" width="1064" height="708" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-43301 lazyload" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAABCgAAALEAQAAAADiRTySAAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAABzSURBVHja7cEBDQAAAMKg909tDjegAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAIBXA3KnAAFLEcMIAAAAAElFTkSuQmCC" alt=""   data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia.jpg 1064w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia-768x511.jpg 768w" data-max-width="1064" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1064" data-eio-rheight="708" /><noscript><img
decoding="async" width="1064" height="708" class="alignnone size-medium wp-image-43301" src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia.jpg" alt="" srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia.jpg 1064w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/museo-del-risorgimento-castello-di-brescia-768x511.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1064px) 100vw, 1064px" data-eio="l" /></noscript><h2 class="text-xl xl:text-3xl text-secondary mb-6 lg:mb-12">The Museo del Risorgimento <i>Leonessa d’Italia</i> reopens its doors for the first time in 20 years in the <b>Grande Miglio</b> of the <b>Castle</b> with an innovative and immersive layout</h2><div
class="prose-ground text-primary mb-6 lg:mb-12"><p>The result of long historiographical and design work, this new exhibition recounts the Risorgimento as a phenomenon of European relevance and of pressing topicality. Paintings, sculptures, heirlooms and “relics”, having undergone careful restorations, are showcased as material manifestations of a long and complex history that culminated in Italian Unification. A rich digital collection accompanies and integrates a storytelling that reaches up to the present day, involving the visitor in the events that saw Brescia at the centre of the long Risorgimento, making it famous throughout the world as the <em>Lioness of Italy</em>.</p></div><p>The route embodies a new concept of historical museums, in which storytelling plays a leading role and takes the audience on a journey from the Risorgimento to current events. The museum alternates local and European scale, narrative and analysis, objects and digital collections, with the aim of illustrating events, places, and protagonists of the Italian Risorgimento in an innovative and participatory way. The exhibition consists of eight sections and is entirely narrated in both Italian and English.</p></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div></div><div
class="container my-16"><div
class="relative"><div
data-video-platform="youtube" data-video-id="x1Gszn47MF0" class="cursor-pointer js-video-trigger"><div
class="aspect-w-16 aspect-h-9 overflow-hidden">
<img
decoding="async" width="1024" height="682" src="data:image/png;base64,iVBORw0KGgoAAAANSUhEUgAABAAAAAKqAQAAAACMMRGcAAAAAnRSTlMAAHaTzTgAAABrSURBVHja7cEBAQAAAIIg/69uSEABAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAAvBtXuQABimusowAAAABJRU5ErkJggg==" class="absolute object-cover w-full lazyload" alt=""   data-src="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Castello-di-Brescia_2.jpg" data-srcset="https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Castello-di-Brescia_2.jpg 1024w, https://www.bresciamusei.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/01/Castello-di-Brescia_2-768x512.jpg 768w" data-max-width="1024" data-sizes="auto" data-eio-rwidth="1024" data-eio-rheight="682" /><noscript><img
